    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "EV_002",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "speaker_role": "patient",
      "transcript_text": "Yes, asthma. I'd rather know what's going on than keep guessing. I've had high blood pressure, yes.",
      "start_timestamp": "02:01",
      "end_timestamp": "02:11",
      "supports": [
        "clinical_conditions[0]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "EV_003",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "speaker_role": "doctor",
      "transcript_text": "Okay: respiratory rate 18, SpO2 97 percent on room air, heart rate 89, temperature is 39.3 degrees, blood pressure 105 over 75.",
      "start_timestamp": "03:38",
      "end_timestamp": "03:49",
      "supports": [
        "vital_signs[0]",
        "physical_examination[0]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "EV_004",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "speaker_role": "doctor",
      "transcript_text": "There are a few scattered wheezes on both sides.",
      "start_timestamp": "04:14",
      "end_timestamp": "04:18",
      "supports": [
        "physical_examination[0]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "EV_005",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "speaker_role": "doctor",
      "transcript_text": "Taking everything into account, this looks most like acute bronchitis. It could also be pneumonia, so we'll keep that open. We should do a chest X-ray for cough. I want blood tests done, that's for cough. I'd rather check than guess, and this is the quickest way to know more.",
      "start_timestamp": "04:57",
      "end_timestamp": "05:20",
      "supports": [
        "procedures_and_investigations[0]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "EV_006",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "speaker_role": "doctor",
      "transcript_text": "Let's use salbutamol inhaler, 100 micrograms, two puffs when needed, for one week. It's for wheeze. I'll put all of this in writing so you don't have to remember it. I'm prescribing azithromycin, 500 milligrams, once a day, for three days. You take it by mouth. It's for chest infection. It's a commonly used medicine and most people tolerate it well. Steam and warm fluids can ease the cough, and try to sleep with your head propped up.",
      "start_timestamp": "05:23",
      "end_timestamp": "06:01",
